\begin_layout Section
Finding Demographic Stability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we describe pseudocode for calculating the demographically stable probability distribution of population densities across patches and of the transition probabilities between patch density states.
 Patches have a population size state 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

.
 The actual population size for any given state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q_{n}=n_{min}\cdot(n+1)\label{eq:}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{min}$
\end_inset

 is the maximum population size of any patch.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Reproduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Adults,
 individuals of size state 
\begin_inset Formula $s=S$
\end_inset

,
 reproduce asexually at a rate dependent on their reproductive quality state.
 We here define 
\begin_inset Formula $R(s,m,n)$
\end_inset

,
 the expected reproductive rate of an individual of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,m\}$
\end_inset

 in a patch of density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R(s,m,n)=\begin{cases}
\text{if \ensuremath{s\geq S} and }m\geq m_{switch}: & \sum_{g}p(g|q_{n})\cdot g\cdot(r_{b}+m\cdot r_{m}+r_{fighter})\\
\text{if \ensuremath{s\geq S} and }m<m_{switch} & \sum_{g}p(g|q_{n})\cdot g\cdot(r_{b}+m\cdot r_{m})\\
\text{else}: & 0
\end{cases}\label{eq:-1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where S is their size at maturity,
 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{switch}$
\end_inset

 is the threshold investment in quality above which the fighter polyphenism is expressed,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{b}$
\end_inset

 is the baseline reproductive rate,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{m}$
\end_inset

 is the reproductive benefit of greater somatic quality,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{fighter}$
\end_inset

 is reproductive benefit of expressing the fighter polymorphism allowing them to extract auxiliary resources for reproduction.
 To make solving the model tractable we must assume the fighter phenotype does not influence competition for the shared limited resource represented by 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Dispersal
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Migrant Physiology
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ideally,
 individuals would be born with precise and accurate prior information regarding the probability distribution over the population density states of patches.
 Individuals would then update their information state given cues for the population density of their patch which come from the availability of food,
 see section 1.2.
 Individuals are born at size state 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 and reproductive quality state 0,
 and a belief state over population densities of
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi\approx\eta_{1}\label{eq:-3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Where 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of patches that are of density state 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{0}=1-\eta_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of patches that are of density state 
\begin_inset Formula $n=0$
\end_inset

.
 However,
 to form a discrete grid of states that we can solve a Markov decision process for we need interpolate each 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 onto a grid of possible discrete probability values,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=i\in\{0/I,1/I,2/I,...,I/I\}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is an integer determining the fineness of the grid.
 The first step of interpolation is to find the floor of 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{1}$
\end_inset

when it is scaled by the grid size,
 which will give us the nearest grid cell 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 below the continuous value
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
i=\lfloor\eta_{1}\cdot I\rfloor,
\]

\end_inset

and subsequently the difference is the fractional part 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\eta_{1}\cdot I-i.
\]

\end_inset

We can then use the fractional part as a probability that the state should be interpolated to be 
\begin_inset Formula $(i+1)$
\end_inset

,
 finally giving us two discrete states and associated probabilities for defining the state distribution of migrants:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{\rho}(s=1,\pi=i,m=0)=1-f,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{\rho}(s=1,\pi=i+1,m=0)=f
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Patch Densities transition probabilities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming a dispersal rate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 and a fixed number of patches 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

,
 the number of individuals arriving in each patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\alpha(q_{n})=(1-\mu)\cdot q_{n}\cdot r(q_{n})+\frac{\mu\cdot\cancel{N}\cdot\sum_{o}[\eta_{o}\cdot q_{o}\cdot r(q_{o})]}{\cancel{N}}\label{eq:-4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $r(q_{n})$
\end_inset

 is the expected reproductive rate of an individual in a patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 and where 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of patches that are of density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 in the numerator of the fraction is there to scale the number of migrants given the number of patches producing them,
 but that 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 gets canceled out as these migrants are dispersed across these patches (see the denominator),
 telling us that the number of immigrants to a patch is not dependent on the number of patches;
 only the frequency distribution over patch states matters.
 The expected reproductive rate of an individual in a patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 is defined as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r(q_{n})=\sum^{S}_{s}\sum^{\Pi}_{\pi}\sum^{M}_{m}[\rho_{n}(s,\pi,m)\cdot R(s,m,n)]\label{eq:-5}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{n}(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

 is the probability that an individual will be of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

 given that they are in a patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R(s,m,n)$
\end_inset

 is the reproductive rate of an individual of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,m\}$
\end_inset

 in a patch of density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 (see section 1.4).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Following arrivals,
 the new population density of patches of prior density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 become 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q^{\prime}_{n}=max(q_{0},min(q_{1},q_{n}+\alpha(q_{n})))\label{eq:-6}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Given that we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the maximum population density and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the minimum population density.
 We must then interpolate the posterior population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{\prime}_{n}$
\end_inset

 back on to the grid of density states,
 with the probability of the posterior population density state being 
\begin_inset Formula $n^{\prime}=1$
\end_inset

 being
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{1}(n)=\frac{q^{\prime}_{n}-n_{low}}{n_{max}}\label{eq:-7}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and the probability that of posterior state 
\begin_inset Formula $n^{\prime}=0$
\end_inset

 being
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{0}(n)=1-p_{1}(n).\label{eq:-10}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This then returns the transition probabilities between patch density states given dispersal 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{d_{1}(n)}|q_{n})$
\end_inset

,
 which at demographic stability will be used for finding the best response strategy.
 For all 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{d_{1}(n)}|q_{n})=0$
\end_inset

 and then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Add 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}(n)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{1}|q_{n})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Add 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{0}(n)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{0}|q_{n})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Projection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The posterior proportion of patches that are of a high population density shall be 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\eta^{\prime}_{1}=\sum_{n}\eta_{1}\cdot p_{births}(q_{1}|q_{n})\label{eq:-11}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with posterior proportion of patches being low density of course being 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{\prime}_{0}=1-\eta^{\prime}_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Regarding the distribution of physiological states within patches,
 individuals in any patch state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 will have a physiological state frequency distribution defined given the transition probabilities between patches and weighted by the change in population size for these transitions.
 Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall i\forall\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\rho^{\prime}_{n}(s,\pi,m)=\sum_{o}[\frac{1}{\eta^{\prime}_{n}}\cdot\eta_{o}\cdot p_{births}(q_{n}|q_{o})\cdot(\frac{q_{o}}{q_{n}}\cdot\rho_{o}(s,\pi,m)+\frac{q_{n}-q_{o}}{q_{n}}\cdot\tilde{\rho}(s,\pi,m))]\label{eq:-12}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where if patch were to transition from a density of o to a density of n,
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\frac{q_{o}}{q_{n}}\cdot\rho_{o}(s,\pi,m)+\frac{q_{n}-q_{o}}{q_{n}}\cdot\tilde{\rho}(s,\pi,m))$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of individuals who are of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

 within that patch.
 This does however indicate that different patches will have different physiological distributions amongst their populations depending on the historical density states of that patch which would violate our assumptions of this being a Markov process,
 so (to maintain tractability) we assume that individuals have equal access to food regardless of physiological state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Foraging
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals observe cues for population density in the form of food availability.
 Food availability is a random variable influenced by the population density,
 all patches produce the same amount of food which must be shared,
 so for any given patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 individuals receive a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g\sim\text{Normal}(\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{n}},\sigma_{g})\label{eq:-14}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{g}$
\end_inset

 is the mean food mass production of any patch and parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{g}$
\end_inset

 is the food mass standard deviation.
 However,
 as we are working with discrete mathematics,
 we must approximate this normal distribution using a pseudo-normal probability distribution over a grid of possible food mass values 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{G}=\{0,1,...,G_{max}\}$
\end_inset

,
 with probabilities for each realised food mass being 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
p(g|q_{n})=\frac{1}{K}e^{-\frac{(g-\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{n}})^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}_{g}}} & \text{for} & g\in\boldsymbol{G},\end{array}\label{eq:-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is a suitable integer that determines the fineness of the grid of values,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
K=\underset{-G}{\overset{G}{\boldsymbol{\sum}}}e^{-\frac{(g-\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{n}})^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}_{g}}}.\label{eq:-15}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Observation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the food mass 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 individuals observe the resulting nutrient data and update their population density information states (i.e.
 their belief states).
 Assuming Bayesian optimal updating,
 their posterior belief is defined 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall i$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi^{\prime}=\frac{\pi\cdot p(g|q_{1})}{\pi\cdot p(g|q_{1})+(1-\pi)\cdot p(g|q_{0})}.\label{eq:-16}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The posterior belief state 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 then needs to be interpolated onto a discrete grid of possible values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 which (because it represent a probability distribution) lies on a unit simplex (i.e.
 all values are positive and they add up to 1),
 requiring a grid of barycentric co-ordinates.
 To this end,
 the first step is to decompose each 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 into it's integer and fractional parts,
 with the integer part defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
i=\lfloor\pi^{\prime}\cdot I\rfloor,
\]

\end_inset

and subsequently the fractional part defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\pi^{\prime}\cdot I-i.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This then gives the transition probabilities between discrete belief states
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(\pi^{\prime}=i|\pi,g)=1-f,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(\pi^{\prime}=i+1|\pi,g)=f.
\]

\end_inset

Finally,
 for all other possible posterior co-ordinates on the belief grid,
 the transition probability is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,g)=0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Development
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each individual,
 choose a developmental action given their state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

,
 given the resident developmental strategy for that state 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,\pi^{\prime},m)\in(0,G)$
\end_inset

.
 Their developmental action determines the threshold mass above which energy foraged goes to the development of phenotypes of greater reproductive quality (e.g.
 fighter morphology),
 generating a transition in their size state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and their reproductive quality state 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
 Of the portion of energy allocated to quality,
 all energy below a size-dependent investment threshold goes to maintenance costs.
 Therefore,
 if investment in quality falls below this threshold,
 given their state-dependent strategy and their food mass,
 there is a discounted probability of survival
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(survival|g,s,\pi^{\prime},m)=min(1,\frac{g-\Delta m-\Delta s}{y(s,m)})\label{eq:-44}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

such that if the individual has no energy to allocate to maintenance they die,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $y(s)$
\end_inset

 is the size dependent maintenance cost 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y(s,m)=\frac{s^{3}\cdot m}{S^{3}\cdot M}\cdot y_{max}\label{eq:-22}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we assume maintenance costs scale by the volume of the individual and by the quality of their somatic cells and developmental systems (given their quality state 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

),
 and where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{max}$
\end_inset

 is the maximum maintenance investment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The resulting transition probabilities,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

,
 between state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime}\}$
\end_inset

 and state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

 given the resident developmental strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

 and discounted survival probabilities,
 are found be doing the following:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m,g\}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(s^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)=0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 and for each state,
 calculate the changes in quality and size
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta m=max(g-x(s,\pi^{\prime},m),0)\label{eq:-20-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta m$
\end_inset

 is the change in quality state of individuals after ingesting a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 and where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta s=max(g-\Delta m-y(s,m),0),\label{eq:-19-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is the change in size state of individuals after ingesting a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 given that all energy allocated to growth below the fixed and state-dependent maintenance cost threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $y(s,m)$
\end_inset

,
 below which all food mass is invested in maintenance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
These changes then need to be interpolated onto the size and quality grids,
 and for all interpolated values we find the resulting discretised transitions and their respective probabilities
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(s+\Delta s,m+\Delta m|s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)=p(\Delta s)\cdot p(\Delta m)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Updating the States Distributions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the baseline rate of death 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 and deaths caused by lack of investment in maintenance,
 the posterior density of each state for patches of each state 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\rho^{\prime}_{n}}(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime})=\sum_{g}\sum_{s}\sum_{\pi}\sum_{m}p(s^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)\cdot P(survival|g,s,\pi^{\prime},m)\cdot p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,g)\cdot p(g|q_{n})\cdot\rho^{\prime}_{n}(s,\pi,m)\label{eq:-17}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where,
 as a reminder,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho^{\prime}_{n}(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

 is the probability distribution over states after dispersal within patches of population size 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p(g|q_{n})$
\end_inset

 is the probability of a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 in patches of size 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,g)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of transitioning to posterior information state 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 given this food mass,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P(survival|s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of survival of maintenance costs given their developmental strategy dependent on posterior information state 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of transitioning to size state 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and quality state 
\begin_inset Formula $m^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 given the outcomes of development and given that they have survived maintenance costs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the baseline rate of death 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 and deaths caused by lack of investment in maintenance,
 patches can transition to lower population density states.
 Therefore,
 given the posterior densities of individuals within patches,
 the posterior of population size of a patch of density state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{o}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(o)=q_{o}\cdot(1-\lambda)\cdot\sum_{s}\sum_{\pi}\sum_{m}\hat{\rho^{\prime}_{o}}(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime})
\]

\end_inset

 This is needed to calculate the posterior distribution of patch states
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\eta^{\hat{\prime}}(q_{n})=\sum_{o\in\{0,1\}}\eta^{\prime}_{o}\cdot p_{\text{fail}}(q_{n}|q_{o})\label{eq:-23}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\text{fail}}(q_{n}|q_{o})$
\end_inset

 is the transition probability of a patch from state 
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 given deaths from maintenance failures,
 defined by interpolating 
\begin_inset Formula $u(o)$
\end_inset

 thus for each 
\begin_inset Formula $o\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{\text{fail}}(q_{1}|q_{o})=\frac{min(max(u(o),q_{0}),q_{1})-n_{low}}{n_{max}}\label{eq:-26}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and then 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{\text{fail}}(q_{0}|q_{o})=1-\frac{min(max(u(o),q_{0}),q_{1})-n_{low}}{n_{max}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The posterior probability distribution over individual states for patches of each population density state is therefore 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\rho^{\prime\prime}_{n}(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime})=\sum_{q_{o}}\frac{1}{\eta^{\hat{\prime}}_{n}}\cdot\eta^{\prime}_{o}\cdot p_{\text{fail}}(q_{n}|q_{o})\cdot\frac{\hat{\rho^{\prime}_{o}}(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime})}{\sum_{s}\sum_{\pi}\sum_{m}\hat{\rho^{\prime}_{o}}(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime})}\label{eq:-30}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Demographic stochasticity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every patch undergoes stochastic perturbations in population density,
 influenced by patch population density (e.g.
 the local extinction of a prey species).
 We assume that the probability of death is equal for all individuals so there is no change in the distribution of individual states within patches as a result of these perturbations.
 We define the resulting transition probabilities as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{patch}(q_{0}|q_{0})=1-\gamma_{low}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{patch}(q_{1}|q_{0})=\gamma_{low}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{patch}(q_{0}|q_{1})=\gamma_{high}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{patch}(q_{1}|q_{1})=1-\gamma_{high}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{low}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{high}$
\end_inset

 give the rates of transition up from low patch density states and down from high density states respectively.
 We assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{high}>\gamma_{low}$
\end_inset

,
 as high density patches are more susceptible to famine and disease outbreaks.
 Given these perturbations,
 the proportion of patches that are of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

 in the next time step is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\eta^{\prime\prime}_{n}q_{n})=\sum_{o\in\{0,1\}}p_{patch}(q_{n}|q_{o})\cdot\eta^{\hat{\prime}}_{o}\label{eq:-35}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and similarly the posterior physiological state distribution for each patch state is 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall n$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho^{\prime\prime}_{n}(s,\pi,m)=\sum_{o\in\{0,1\}}\frac{1}{\eta^{\prime\prime}_{n}}\cdot p_{patch}(q_{n}|q_{o})\cdot\eta^{\hat{\prime}}_{o}\cdot\rho^{\prime\prime}_{o}(s,\pi,m).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Belief updating between growth cycles
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals also know the transition probabilities between patch population density states at demographic stability in their evolutionary history,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{p}(q_{n}|q_{o})$
\end_inset

,
 resulting from these growth and death processes,
 and so individuals that survive to the next growth cycle will update their information states accordingly for all 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi^{\prime}=\pi\cdot\tilde{p}(q_{1}|q_{1})+(1-\pi)\cdot\tilde{p}(q_{1}|q_{0}).\label{eq:-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

For the first instance,
 when we have not yet found demographic stability,
 we will assume that their priors are that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall n\forall o,\tilde{p}(q_{n}|q_{o})=0$
\end_inset

,
 as this should not have a strong influence on the long term adaptive dynamics assuming that the dynamics are ordered rather than chaotic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Demographic Stability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming that with successive generations 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 that the distribution of patches over states converges on stable values,
 i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\text{\ensuremath{\eta^{*}_{1}}=lim}_{\rightarrow\infty}[\eta_{1}]\label{eq:-36}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

further,
 we can approximate 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{*}_{1}$
\end_inset

 by iterating over generations until 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
tol>|\eta_{1}-\eta^{\prime\prime}_{1}|\label{eq:-37}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the tolerance threshold,
 
\begin_inset Formula $tol$
\end_inset

,
 is very small,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{\prime\prime}_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of patches that are of population density state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{1}$
\end_inset

 after dispersal and deaths.
 Once demographic stability is reached we assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{*}_{1}=\eta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and proceed with invasion analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Finding Evolutionary Stability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Having found the demographically stable state of the metapopulation,
 given the resident strategy,
 we can then use the Bellman equation to find the best response strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $b(x(s,\pi,m))$
\end_inset

 for an invading individual.
 We then assume the best response strategy becomes the new resident strategy,
 and recalculate the demographically stable state of the metapopulation.
 The evolutionarily stable strategy (ESS) is a resident strategy for which invading individuals can do no better than to follow the resident strategy and so the ESS is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bar{x}(s,\pi,m)=b(x(s,\pi,m))=x(s,\pi,m).\label{eq:-38-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Assuming there is a singular ESSs,
 we can iterating over invasions till the best response strategy converges on the ESS,
 and the starting point of the population will not matter.
 By sampling from multiple starting points we can test this assumption,
 and if necessary plot out the adaptive dynamics with phase portraits where there are multiple ESSs or oscillatory dynamics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find the best response we define a Bellman fitness equation for individuals in the model where 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{\prime}(s,\pi,m)=0$
\end_inset

 for all state combinations (as the individual is dead),
 and iterate backwards to the previous time step
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V^{\prime}(s,\pi,m)=R(s,m)+\text{max}_{x(s,\pi,m)=\kappa}[\sum_{s^{\prime}}\sum_{\pi^{\prime\prime}}\sum_{m^{\prime}}a(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi,m,\kappa)\cdot V(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime\prime},m^{\prime})],\label{eq:-39-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $R(s,m)$
\end_inset

 is the rate of reproduction for an individual of size 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and quality 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 (see equation 1),
 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{pert}(survival|\pi_{n})$
\end_inset

 is the probability the individual survives between time-steps given patch quality perturbations,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime\prime},m^{\prime},survives|s,\pi,m,\kappa)$
\end_inset

 is the survival (given maintenance) and state transition probability to the posterior state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime\prime},m^{\prime}\}$
\end_inset

 of individuals of prior state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
State transitions between developmental actions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals are born with accurate prior information regarding the probability distribution over the population density states of patches 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi=\eta_{1}\label{eq:-3-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals have a probability of death from patch depletion and a probability of death 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 from exogenous factors (e.g.
 old age).
 Given an individual's information state after foraging 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\pi}^{\prime}=\{\pi_{j=1},\pi_{j=2},...,\pi_{j=I}\}$
\end_inset

 and given the probability of patch density transition at demographic stability,
 the probability that an individual dies due to patch depletion is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{pert}(survival|\boldsymbol{\pi})=(1-\lambda)\cdot\sum_{j}\sum_{k}\pi_{j}\cdot(1-p_{pert}(q_{k}|q_{j})\cdot\frac{q_{j}-q_{k}}{q_{j}})\label{eq:-45}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Individuals also know the transition probabilities between patch population density states at demographic stability given patch perturbations and dispersal,
 since the previous development action.
 The transition probability at demographic stability of a patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 to a density of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
 given perturbations and dispersal is full therefore,
 the posterior belief 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi^{\prime}=(1-\pi)\cdot\sum_{q_{j}}\tilde{p}_{births}(q_{1}|q_{j})\cdot\tilde{p}_{births}(q_{j}|q_{0})+\pi\cdot\sum_{q_{j}}\tilde{p}_{births}(q_{1}|q_{j})\cdot\tilde{p}_{births}(q_{j}|q_{1})
\]

\end_inset

which then has to be interpolated on the discrete belief state space as before,
 with the integer part defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
i=\lfloor\pi^{\prime}\cdot I\rfloor,
\]

\end_inset

and subsequently the fractional part defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\pi^{\prime}\cdot I-i.
\]

\end_inset

We can then generate the transition probability matrix for belief states given that population growth and patch perturbations will have occurred within the time between developmental actions,
 i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,time)$
\end_inset

,
 defined by running the following algorithm for all prior belief states:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,time)=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\text{add \ensuremath{(1-f)} to }p(\pi^{\prime}=i|\pi,time)$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\text{add \ensuremath{f} to }p(\pi^{\prime}=i+1|\pi,time)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The posterior belief 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is then updated again upon receiving a food mass of g for each of 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\{0,1,2\}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi^{\prime\prime}_{i}=\frac{\pi^{\prime}\cdot p(g|q_{1})}{\pi^{\prime}\cdot p(g|q_{1})+(1-\pi^{\prime})\cdot p(g|q_{0})}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which then has to be interpolated on the discrete belief state space as before,
 with the integer part defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
i=\lfloor\pi^{\prime}\cdot I\rfloor,
\]

\end_inset

and subsequently the fractional part defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\pi^{\prime}\cdot I-i.
\]

\end_inset

We can then generate the transition probability matrix for belief states given a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 for each prior belief state 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\pi^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\pi^{\prime\prime}|\pi^{\prime},g)=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\text{add \ensuremath{(1-f)} to }p(\pi^{\prime\prime}=i|\pi^{\prime},g)$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\text{add \ensuremath{f}to }p(\pi^{\prime\prime}=i+1|\pi^{\prime},g)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
State Transitions During Development
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As observations of patch density and an individual's development are linked by the same random variable,
 food mass 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 we define the state transition probability given individual decision making as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
a(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi,m,\kappa)=P_{pert}(survival|\boldsymbol{\pi})\cdot\sum_{\pi^{\prime}}[p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,time)\cdot\sum_{g}[p(g|\pi^{\prime})\cdot p(\pi^{\prime\prime}|\pi^{\prime},g)\cdot p(s^{\prime},m^{\prime},survives|\kappa,s,m,\pi^{\prime\prime},g)]]\label{eq:-40}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s^{\prime},m^{\prime},survives|\kappa,s,m,\pi^{\prime\prime},g)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of posterior size 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and quality 
\begin_inset Formula $m^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 given the individual's developmental policy dependent on their current size,
 quality,
 and information states,
 given the food mass they received,
 and given their probability of survival given maintenance costs,
 all defined once by doing the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m,g,\kappa\}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(s^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi,m,g,\kappa)=0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and all 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 define the transitions in state predicted by their developmental strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa,$
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta m=max(g-\kappa,0)\label{eq:-20-1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is the change in quality state of individuals after ingesting a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 and where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta s=max(g-\Delta m-y(s,m),0),\label{eq:-19-1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is the change in size state of individuals after ingesting a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 given that all energy allocated to growth below the fixed and state-dependent maintenance cost threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $y(s,m)$
\end_inset

,
 below which all food mass is invested in maintenance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Then add 
\begin_inset Formula $p(survival|g,s,m,\kappa)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s+\Delta s,m+\Delta m,survives|s,\pi,m,g,\kappa)$
\end_inset

,
 where depending on the food mass 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 an individual encounters and the fraction of that mass that they invest in maintenance,
 there is a probability of death from lack of maintenance of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(survival|g,s,m,\kappa)=min(1,\frac{g-\Delta m-\Delta s}{y(s,m)})\label{eq:-44-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y(s,m)$
\end_inset

 is the size and quality dependent maintenance cost threshold to avoid death completely,
 defined as shown previously:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y(s,m)=\frac{s^{3}\cdot m}{S^{3}\cdot M}\cdot g_{max}\label{eq:-22-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Appendix:
 Age and Development
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now that we have found the state-dependent evolutionarily stable strategy for a given set of parameter values,
 along with the associated demographically stable distribution of individual physiological states and patch density states,
 we can ask what the ESS behaviour of a cohort of individuals within such a population would look like.
 We can assume that one small cohort following the same ESS will not have an influence on the distribution of states of the larger population and so we do not need to update any of the transition probabilities.
 We know that they will begin life at the minimum size and quality states and with their belief state being the evolved prior for the stable patch distribution prior to dispersal,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{*}_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Where 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{*}_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of patches prior to dispersal that are of density state 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{0}=1-\eta_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of patches that are of density state 
\begin_inset Formula $n=0$
\end_inset

.
 However,
 to form a discrete grid of states that we can solve a Markov decision process for we need interpolate each 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 onto a grid of possible discrete probability values,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=i\in\{0/I,1/I,2/I,...,I/I\}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is an integer determining the fineness of the grid.
 The first step of interpolation is to find the floor of 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{*}_{1}$
\end_inset

when it is scaled by the grid size,
 which will give us the nearest grid cell 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 below the continuous value
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
i=\lfloor\eta_{1}\cdot I\rfloor,
\]

\end_inset

and subsequently the difference is the fractional part 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\eta_{1}\cdot I-i.
\]

\end_inset

We can then use the fractional part as a probability that the state should be interpolated to be 
\begin_inset Formula $(i+1)$
\end_inset

,
 finally giving us two discrete states and associated probabilities for defining the state distribution of this cohort as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{i,t}(s,\pi=i,m)=1-f
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{i,t}(s,\pi=i+1,m)=f
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

